Cut a thin slice of a lizard bone and you see slow, neat growth rings. Most dinosaur bones look different. They are full of channels where blood vessels ran, a type of bone that forms when an animal grows quickly. Fast growth needs plenty of energy, and that needs a fast-burning body.

There is a clever chemical test too. As an animal makes an eggshell, the heat of its body leaves a tiny signature in the way certain [[atom|atoms]] are arranged. The shell works like a [[@thermometer|thermometer]] frozen in stone. In 2015, scientists tested [[fossil|fossil]] eggshells this way. A titanosaur from Argentina came out at about 38°C, close to a human. A smaller oviraptor-like dinosaur from Mongolia came out cooler, around 32°C.

{{story:penguins-cold}}

T. rex belonged to the tyrannosaurs, a family that lived mainly in North America and Asia. Rajasaurus was an abelisaur, a family that lived mainly on the southern continents: South America, Africa, Madagascar and India.

These families had been separate for a very long time. Yet both became the top hunters in their lands, and both had big heads and very short arms. When different families evolve similar bodies for a similar way of life, scientists call it convergent [[evolution|evolution]].

{{story:trex-arms}}

Flying fish leap mainly to escape predators such as tuna, marlin and dolphinfish. Underwater, a hunter loses track of a fish that suddenly vanishes into the air. When the fish lands, it may be many metres away.

But the air has its own hunters. Seabirds such as frigatebirds sometimes snatch flying fish in mid-glide. Some flying fish land on the decks of ships at night, attracted by lights or carried by the wind, and sailors have long collected them for breakfast.

{{story:gliders}}

That spring, unusually many icebergs had drifted south from [[@arctic|the Arctic]]. Icebergs break off [[@glaciers|glaciers]], mostly in [[@world-greenland|Greenland]], and float south on the cold Labrador Current. Several ships radioed warnings about ice to the Titanic that day. Not all of them reached the captain.

The night was calm and moonless. With no waves breaking at its base, the iceberg was hard to spot until it was very close. The lookouts in the crow's nest had no binoculars. By the time they shouted the warning, there was less than a minute to turn. The ship swerved but scraped along the ice.

{{story:floating-ships}}

Early sheep had a coat of coarse hair, with only a little soft wool underneath, like their wild cousins. Over thousands of years, herders chose to breed the sheep with the softest, longest fleeces. Woolly sheep spread across West Asia several thousand years after sheep were first tamed.

Wool changed history. It could be spun into thread, woven into warm cloth and traded. In [[medieval|medieval]] England, wool was so important to the country's wealth that the Lord Speaker in Parliament still sits on a large cushion stuffed with wool, called the Woolsack. Unlike wild animals, modern woolly sheep don't shed their coat by themselves, so they must be sheared every year. The fleece then becomes [[@wool|wool]] for blankets, shawls and sweaters.

{{story:farming-began}}

Nature made glass long before people did. When some volcanoes erupt, their [[lava|lava]] cools so fast that it forms a shiny black glass called obsidian. [[@stone-age|Stone Age]] people chipped it into blades sharper than steel scalpels.

When [[@lightning|lightning]] strikes sandy ground, it can melt the sand into hollow, branching tubes of glass called fulgurites. In the [[@sahara|Sahara]], there is a pale yellow glass scattered across the desert. It is about 29 million years old, and many scientists think it formed when a space rock struck or exploded above the desert.

{{story:volcano}}

The [[@drums|drum]] is the heart of bhangra. A dhol is a barrel-shaped wooden drum with skin on both ends, hung from the player's shoulder. One side is struck with a thick, curved stick for a deep boom. The other is hit with a thin cane for a sharp crack.

Other instruments join in. The tumbi is a one-string instrument with a bright, twanging sound. The algoza is a pair of wooden flutes played together, and the chimta is a pair of long iron tongs with jingles, which is clapped and shaken.

{{story:partition}}

The Lok Sabha has 543 elected members, each representing one constituency. Bigger states have more seats, so Uttar Pradesh has 80 and small states like [[@sikkim|Sikkim]] and Mizoram have one each. Members must be at least 25 years old. The number of seats each state gets is still based on the 1971 [[census|census]], so that states which slowed their population growth would not lose seats. It is due to be redrawn after the first census taken after 2026.

The Lok Sabha is elected for five years, unless it is [[dissolve|dissolved]] early. Whichever party or alliance has a majority, at least 272 seats, forms the government. It is chaired by the Speaker, who keeps order and decides who may speak. The Speaker is expected to be fair to all sides, even though they are elected as a member of a party.

{{story:congress-usa}}

The [[@united-nations|United Nations]] was set up in 1945, after the Second World War, to prevent future wars. In 1948, it sent unarmed military observers to the Middle East to watch over a truce. That was the first peacekeeping mission, and it still operates today.

The first armed peacekeeping force came in 1956, during the Suez Crisis in Egypt. Its soldiers needed to be recognised at a glance, so blue helmet covers were quickly made, using the UN's own colour. In 1988, UN peacekeepers as a whole were awarded the [[@nobel-peace|Nobel Peace Prize]].

{{story:united-nations}}

India has not signed the 1951 Convention, but it has taken in huge numbers of people. During [[@partition|Partition]] in 1947, about 1.5 crore people crossed the new borders between [[@pakistan-history|India and Pakistan]], in both directions. Many arrived with nothing.

In 1959, the Dalai Lama fled Tibet and came to India, followed by thousands of Tibetans. Their community at McLeod Ganj, above Dharamshala in [[@himachal|Himachal Pradesh]], is still home to many of them. In 1971, during the war that created [[@bangladesh|Bangladesh]], about a crore people fled into India.

{{story:partition}}

An old story from India tells of the clever inventor of [[@chess-india|chess]]. The king was so pleased that he offered any reward. The inventor asked for rice: one grain on the first square of the chessboard, two on the second, four on the third, doubling each time, for all 64 squares.

The king laughed at such a small request. But the numbers grow as powers of 2. The 11th square has 1,024 grains. The 21st has over a million. The 64th square alone would need 2⁶³ grains, about 9 quintillion. All 64 squares together come to more [[@rice|rice]] than the whole world would harvest in hundreds of years. The story is a legend, but the maths is real.

{{story:rice}}

Consider the sentence "This sentence is false." If it's true, then it's false. If it's false, then it's true. This is the liar paradox, and philosophers have argued about it since ancient Greek times.

In 1901, the British thinker Bertrand Russell found a version inside maths itself. A simple form goes like this. A village barber shaves everyone who does not shave themselves. Who shaves the barber? Either answer contradicts the rule. Russell's discovery forced mathematicians to rebuild the rules of [[@sets-venn|sets]] more carefully.

{{story:optical-illusions}}

In Meghalaya's capital, [[@shillong|Shillong]], [[@choirs|church choirs]], rock records and guitar lessons are part of everyday life. The singer Lou Majaw has held a free concert on Bob Dylan's birthday every May since the 1970s.

{{story:shillong}}

Bands from Shillong and the rest of Northeast India often win national rock contests. The Hornbill Festival in Nagaland hosts a big rock contest every December.

In 1950, on a work trip to London, Ray watched about a hundred films in a few months. One Italian film, *Bicycle Thieves*, showed him that a great movie could be made with real streets and actors who were not stars.

Back home, he started filming *Pather Panchali* with a camera operator who had never shot a film and many actors who had never been in a film. He kept his day job and filmed at weekends. The money ran out, and shooting stopped for many months, until the [[@governors-states|state government]] stepped in. Ray began the famous scene of Apu and his sister running through white kash flowers to see a train. When he came back to finish it, cattle had eaten the flowers, so he waited a year for them to bloom again.

{{story:tagore}}

जब कोई बर्फ़ीली चीज़ तालू से टकराती है, तो वहाँ की नन्ही खून की नलियाँ गर्मी बचाने के लिए झट से सिकुड़ जाती हैं। फिर वे अचानक चौड़ी हो जाती हैं, ताकि गर्म खून तेज़ी से वापस आ सके। कई वैज्ञानिक मानते हैं कि यही तेज़ सिकुड़ना और फूलना खतरे की घंटी बजाता है।

संदेश ट्राइजेमिनल नस से होकर जाता है। यह एक बड़ी नस है जो चेहरे, दाँतों और माथे का एहसास ढोती है। दिमाग को इस नस से तेज़ संकेत मिलता है और वह मान लेता है कि दर्द माथे में ही होगा, क्योंकि ऐसे संकेत आम तौर पर वहीं से आते हैं। यह तुम्हारे [[@nervous-system|तंत्रिका तंत्र]] का एक छोटा रास्ता है, और ज़्यादातर समय यह बिल्कुल ठीक काम करता है।

{{story:body-funny-bone}}

In 2016 a team at the University of St Andrews in Scotland created a kind of deja vu in the lab. They read people lists of words like "bed", "pillow", "night" and "dream", but never "sleep". Later, people felt they had heard "sleep", while also knowing they hadn't.

Brain scans showed that the busiest parts were in the front of the brain, which helps make decisions, not the memory areas. The scientists think deja vu may be a sign of a healthy checking system. Your brain spots a false sense of familiarity and flags it as an error.

{{story:optical-illusions}}

In 2014 the government launched the Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana, a drive to open free [[@money-banks|bank accounts]] for people who had none. Within a few years, hundreds of millions were opened. By 2023 there were more than 50 crore of these accounts.

With an account linked to Aadhaar, the government could send money like [[@pensions-old-age|pensions]], scholarships and gas subsidies straight to people, instead of through layers of officials. This is called direct benefit transfer, and it cut out a lot of middlemen.

{{story:money-banks}}

During training, the model reads a passage with the next token hidden. "The batsman hit the ball over the..." Is the next word "boundary", "fence" or "moon"? The model gives each option a [[probability|probability]]. Then it sees the real answer and adjusts.

The design that made this work so well is called the transformer, described by researchers at Google in 2017. It lets the model look back across a whole passage and decide which earlier words matter most for the next guess. In "The girl who lost her dog was sad because she missed it", the model learns to link "it" back to "dog", not "girl".

{{story:alan-turing}}

Edison's side fought back with fear. They published warnings that AC's high voltages were deadly and staged public demonstrations meant to make AC look dangerous. High-voltage AC could indeed be lethal, and some accidents with overhead wires horrified the public. But DC at high voltage is also dangerous. The campaign was as much about business as safety.

It didn't work. AC was simply better for big networks. In 1892, Edison's company merged with a rival to form General Electric, and Edison lost control of it. The new company soon began building AC systems too.

{{story:niagara-falls}}

New arrivals carried their bags up a long staircase into the Great Hall. As they climbed, doctors watched. Those who seemed ill were marked with chalk and taken aside. Doctors checked eyes for an [[infection|infection]] called trachoma, which could cause blindness, by flipping up eyelids with a small hook.

Then came questions. What is your name? Where are you going? Do you have work or family waiting? Do you have money? There were about 29 questions in all. Interpreters helped, because people spoke dozens of languages.

{{story:statue-liberty}}

Behind the Phoenician coast rose mountains covered in tall cedar trees. Cedar wood was strong and smelled sweet, and the treeless lands of [[@ancient-egypt|Egypt]] and Mesopotamia wanted it badly for ships, temples and palace beams.

The most prized Phoenician product came from a sea snail called the murex. Crushed in huge numbers and left to rot in vats, the snails gave a deep purple dye that did not fade. It took thousands of snails to dye a single cloak. That made purple cloth so costly that in later centuries, Roman emperors wore it as a sign of power. The Greek name for these people may come from a word for this purple-red colour.

{{story:alphabet-history}}

कोई पौधा अपने फल को तीखा क्यों बनाएगा? तीखापन कैप्सेसिन से आता है, जिससे स्तनधारियों का मुँह ऐसा लगता है मानो जल रहा हो। इससे चूहे, गिलहरियाँ और हिरण दूर रहते हैं। ये बीजों को चबाकर नष्ट कर देते हैं।

चिड़ियाँ अलग हैं। उनके गर्मी पहचानने वाले [[sensor|सेंसर]] कैप्सेसिन को अनदेखा कर देते हैं, इसलिए गौरैया सबसे तीखी मिर्च भी बिना किसी झंझट के खा लेती है। चिड़ियाँ बीज साबुत निगलती हैं, उड़ जाती हैं और उन्हें कहीं दूर गिरा देती हैं, अक्सर साथ में थोड़ी-सी खाद के साथ। 2001 में अमेरिका के एरिज़ोना में जंगली मिर्चों पर हुए एक अध्ययन में वैज्ञानिकों ने पाया कि कुतरने वाले जीव जो बीज खाते थे, वे नष्ट हो जाते थे, जबकि चिड़ियों के पेट से होकर निकले बीज फिर भी अंकुरित हो जाते थे। चटक लाल रंग चिड़ियों के लिए लगाए गए विज्ञापन जैसा है, और चिड़ियाँ लाल रंग बहुत अच्छे से देखती हैं।

{{story:spicy-hot}}

Making a good puffing roti is quiet physics. The dough must be soft, with enough water, and rested so the gluten can relax. It must be rolled to an even thickness, because a thin spot will tear and leak steam.

On the tawa, the first side is cooked lightly and the second side a little more, so both have a skin. On the flame, the heat is so strong that water near the middle boils almost at once. The same thing happens in a [[@pressure-cooker|pressure cooker]], where trapped steam builds force until the [[@whistles|whistle]] blows.

{{story:boiling-water}}
